WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.440 --> 00:00:04.460
This call is from a federal prison.

00:00:04.460 --> 00:00:08.920
literally, this prison is surrounded by marijuana
farms

00:00:08.920 --> 00:00:14.400
and the people who work here pass farms on
a daily basis to get to work here.

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:15.800
You know, I struggle with it every morning.

00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:19.880
I mean, I listen to the radio, I hear commercials
about, uh, you know, uh, marijuana businesses,

00:00:19.880 --> 00:00:22.560
you know, every day I wake up and I go, how
did this happen?

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:24.140
You know, why is this happening?

00:00:24.140 --> 00:00:28.680
As I'm sitting here fighting in a state where
it's literally everywhere,

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:33.660
but yet the government is still pursuing me
and biting me as if I was the only one doing it.

00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:35.680
I mean it absolutely blows my mind.

00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:41.520
Colorado was the first state to legalize marijuana
and most voters don't seem to regret it.

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:45.820
Anything less than an ounce, no longer illegal
in Washington State.

00:00:45.829 --> 00:00:50.440
On Thursday Oregon will become the next state
to legalize recreational marijuana.

00:00:50.440 --> 00:00:52.780
California will become the eighth state-

00:00:52.780 --> 00:00:55.579
-people's opinion on this issue have evolved.

00:00:55.579 --> 00:00:58.110
And I felt myself, for like any other American,

00:00:58.110 --> 00:01:01.059
my position evolving over the years.

00:01:01.059 --> 00:01:04.460
Aaron Sandusky has been in federal prison
for almost seven years

00:01:04.460 --> 00:01:07.340
for conspiracy to distribute cannabis.

00:01:07.340 --> 00:01:10.860
He’s one of about 20,000 federal or state
inmates serving time

00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:15.600
for an activity that’s legal in 33 US states
and Washington, D.C.

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:20.120
But there’s a bill working it’s way through
the Senate that might help people like Sandusky

00:01:20.130 --> 00:01:24.729
by expunging their records, and some advocates
believe that President Trump

00:01:24.729 --> 00:01:30.740
may be close to granting clemency to some
of these men and women--including Sandusky.

00:01:30.740 --> 00:01:33.369
I'm not pleading out, I have no intention
of pleading out,

00:01:33.369 --> 00:01:39.399
I'm not concerned about that because I'm going
to defend what I believe is right.

00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:43.660
If I have to go to jail for twenty years defending,
then so be it.

00:01:43.660 --> 00:01:46.729
A thousand marijuana plants and a couple weapons
seized in a raid today,

00:01:46.729 --> 00:01:48.240
this happening in Sacramento County-

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:51.779
A Modesto Police Department narcotics team,
raided a marijuana grow operation -

00:01:51.779 --> 00:01:53.990
One of the reasons we are making these announcements today

00:01:53.990 --> 00:01:58.899
is to try to put to rest the notion that large
marijuana businesses

00:01:58.900 --> 00:02:04.380
can shelter themselves under state law and
operate without fear of federal enforcement.

00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:08.759
In 2011, the Obama administration carried
out a series of federal raids

00:02:08.760 --> 00:02:11.340
on medical marijuana clinics in California,

00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:14.959
despite earlier assurances from the president
and his attorney general

00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:18.760
that they wouldn’t target operators legal
under state law.

00:02:18.760 --> 00:02:21.540
What I'm not going to be doing is is using
justice department resources

00:02:21.549 --> 00:02:23.920
to circumvent state laws on this issue.

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:27.220
What the president said during the campaign,
you will be surprised to know

00:02:27.220 --> 00:02:31.280
will be consistent with what we're doing here
in law enforcement.

00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:37.340
It's been hard, really hard. I'm tired. I just want it to be over.

00:02:37.340 --> 00:02:41.200
He just wanted to find access for people that
could really benefit from it.

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:44.000
My dad died from a brain tumor

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:50.160
and I noticed a lot of patients with cancer
were gravitating towards that as an option for them

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:53.140
to get relief from radiation and treatment.

00:02:53.140 --> 00:02:55.580
This call is from a federal prison.

00:02:55.580 --> 00:02:58.340
So it was something that was close to my heart.

00:02:58.340 --> 00:03:00.140
It was a good option for a lot of patients

00:03:00.140 --> 00:03:05.460
who thought, you know, this is something,
that you know, I feel comfortable exploring.

00:03:05.460 --> 00:03:10.800
I got into it shortly after Obama said he
was not going to use government resources.

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:14.500
When Sandusky opened a marijuana dispensary
in 2009,

00:03:14.520 --> 00:03:18.360
medical cannabis had been legal for thirteen
years in California.

00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:22.680
He located in the town of Upland, which is
one of a handful of municipalities

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:26.920
that was attempting to use the zoning code
to keep the industry away.

00:03:26.920 --> 00:03:31.379
Sandusky alleges that in 2011 then-mayor,
John Pomierski

00:03:31.380 --> 00:03:35.760
demanded a $20,000 bribe to allow him to continue
operating.

00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:52.159
And apparently he wasn't the only business
that he was trying to extort from.

00:03:52.160 --> 00:03:55.840
So there was quite a bit of corruption going
on in that city at the time, which we found out later.

00:03:55.840 --> 00:03:59.080
Sandusky ended up cooperating with the FBI,

00:03:59.080 --> 00:04:04.520
which later arrested and charged Pomierski
with bribery and extortion.

00:04:04.520 --> 00:04:11.159
After Pomierski was indicted, Upland continued
the fight to force Sandusky to close his dispensary.

00:04:11.160 --> 00:04:13.740
So he sued in state court and won.

00:04:13.740 --> 00:04:17.200
But Marijuana is still a schedule one narcotic.

00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:20.600
And according to Sandusky, city officials
retaliated

00:04:20.600 --> 00:04:25.760
by sending a formal request to the US Attorney
to shut down his operations.

00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:31.660
On November 1, 2011 federal agents raided
Sandusky’s businesses.

00:04:31.660 --> 00:04:36.310
They took and cut all the plants down, took
all the lights, cut out all the ballasts,

00:04:36.310 --> 00:04:39.140
took a lot of the equipment that was worth
a value

00:04:39.140 --> 00:04:43.470
and then all our paperwork, our corporate
paperwork which we had here,

00:04:43.470 --> 00:04:46.240
uh, our patient files they seized.

00:04:47.240 --> 00:04:51.340
The federal government charged him with six
counts of drug trafficking.

00:04:51.340 --> 00:04:54.060
Sandusky's employees all took plea deals.

00:04:54.060 --> 00:04:58.640
His business partner testified against him
in return for a lighter sentence.

00:04:58.740 --> 00:05:03.100
Sandusky declined a plea offer that would
have turned him into an informant.

00:05:45.760 --> 00:05:49.000
Sandusky’s attorney was planning to argue
entrapment

00:05:49.020 --> 00:05:52.990
on the grounds that the Obama administration
had publicly stated

00:05:52.990 --> 00:05:55.880
that it wouldn’t prosecute marijuana operators.

00:05:55.880 --> 00:06:00.360
The judge prohibited that line of defense,
and Sandusky was convicted

00:06:00.360 --> 00:06:04.340
and sentenced to the mandatory minimum of
10 years in prison.

00:06:04.340 --> 00:06:07.980
During sentencing, Judge Percy Anderson accused
him

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:12.000
of having lost his “way about what’s right
and what’s wrong.”

00:06:26.060 --> 00:06:28.800
He has bad days and some better days.

00:06:28.800 --> 00:06:30.240
I wouldn't say any good days.

00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:33.740
He's still angry, he's still very, very angry,

00:06:33.740 --> 00:06:37.040
which is concerning because it's been a long
time.

00:06:37.040 --> 00:06:42.410
Like I don't know if he's ever gonna let her
go, he just knows that it was wrong.

00:06:42.410 --> 00:06:49.120
Aaron maybe more than anyone should receive
clemency

00:06:49.120 --> 00:06:54.280
because his case is so disturbing to me.

00:06:54.280 --> 00:06:57.220
Amy Povah did nine years in prison on a drug
charge

00:06:57.220 --> 00:07:00.520
before receiving clemency from Bill Clinton
in 2000.

00:07:00.520 --> 00:07:03.720
Now she runs the the non-profit CAN-DO Foundation,

00:07:03.720 --> 00:07:07.360
which seeks clemency for all nonviolent drug
offenders.

00:07:07.360 --> 00:07:12.800
Sandusky is one of 22 “pot prisoners”
that Can-Do is working to help free.

00:07:12.800 --> 00:07:17.420
We're asking President Trump for his next
act of mercy

00:07:17.420 --> 00:07:22.380
to be strictly for Marijuana cases as a category.

00:07:22.380 --> 00:07:27.420
corporations are making millions and, and
even we're getting into the billions

00:07:27.420 --> 00:07:34.760
it's almost like a human rights violation
to punish people for marijuana offenses

00:07:34.760 --> 00:07:37.880
Others on Povah’s list include Patricia
Albright,

00:07:37.890 --> 00:07:40.340
who started growing and selling medical marijuana

00:07:40.340 --> 00:07:44.120
after discovering that it helped her cancer-stricken
son.

00:07:44.120 --> 00:07:50.920
And Luke Scarmazzo, who petitioned President
Obama for clemency in 2015 but was denied.

00:07:50.920 --> 00:07:54.200
At it's heart, America is a nation of second
chances

00:07:54.210 --> 00:07:58.150
and I believe these folks deserve their second
chance.

00:07:58.150 --> 00:08:01.500
Obama pardoned almost 2,000 federal prisoners,

00:08:01.500 --> 00:08:05.810
but Povah says his legacy on cannabis is decidedly
mixed.

00:08:05.810 --> 00:08:09.030
I started seeing some of the best people denied

00:08:09.030 --> 00:08:12.390
and I started seeing some very questionable
people being granted.

00:08:12.390 --> 00:08:16.700
We really needed to get more people out by
the end of his administration.

00:08:16.700 --> 00:08:18.090
And what would he have to lose?

00:08:18.090 --> 00:08:23.840
I mean, he's leaving and there were just so
many good applicants that got crushed.

00:08:23.840 --> 00:08:28.340
Three years after Obama left the White House,
Povah is feeling hopeful.

00:08:28.340 --> 00:08:32.980
I've been to the Trump White House four
times.

00:08:32.980 --> 00:08:36.310
I was there for Jared Kushner's prison reform
summit.

00:08:36.310 --> 00:08:39.880
They seem to be very aware that having the
office of the partner attorney

00:08:39.880 --> 00:08:43.820
within the clutches of the Department of Justice
is a conflict of interest.

00:08:43.820 --> 00:08:47.740
What I like about president Trump is that
he is an outsider

00:08:47.740 --> 00:08:52.010
and he seems to be the only president who
has been willing to listen

00:08:52.010 --> 00:08:56.340
to those of us in the clemency space who are
considered experts.

00:09:12.200 --> 00:09:15.720
His name has been in front of feel the president's
desk several times.

00:09:15.720 --> 00:09:20.980
So it feels like it's a little, you know,
more possible that it could happen.

00:09:20.980 --> 00:09:25.980
I am predicting that t the cannabis people
who are honest about this will see

00:09:25.980 --> 00:09:28.460
that he's the one that can get this done

00:09:29.760 --> 00:09:35.320
Dana Rohrabacher is a former U.S. Congressman,
a marijuana legalization advocate,

00:09:35.320 --> 00:09:36.620
and a Trump supporter.

00:09:36.620 --> 00:09:40.700
He co-authored the 2014 Rohrabacher-Farr Amendment

00:09:40.700 --> 00:09:45.520
which prohibits the federal government from
expending resources to raid and prosecute

00:09:45.520 --> 00:09:49.860
anyone operating a marijuana-related business
under state law.

00:09:49.860 --> 00:09:55.060
We Republicans claim to base our decisions
on individual freedom,

00:09:55.060 --> 00:09:59.240
state's rights as mandated by the Tenth Amendment
to the Constitution

00:09:59.240 --> 00:10:02.500
and especially the doctor-patient relationship.

00:10:02.500 --> 00:10:07.660
Don't bother to use rhetoric about those principles
on other issues,

00:10:07.660 --> 00:10:13.200
if you vote for the federal government to
supersede individual rights, states rights,

00:10:13.200 --> 00:10:18.340
and the doctor-patient relationship when it
comes to marijuana.

00:10:18.340 --> 00:10:20.860
According to Rohrabacher, under his law,

00:10:20.860 --> 00:10:26.000
Sandusky should be released immediately, though
the courts don’t see it that way.

00:10:26.000 --> 00:10:30.720
they should be let out of jail and they should
be given their freedom, clemency

00:10:30.720 --> 00:10:33.580
and, uh, maybe even an apology.

00:10:33.980 --> 00:10:37.800
Sandusky is set for early release from prison
this November,

00:10:37.800 --> 00:10:41.040
when he’ll be transferred to a halfway house
in California

00:10:41.040 --> 00:10:44.540
and then out on probation in early 2020.

00:10:44.540 --> 00:10:47.160
Even though Sandusky will be out soon,

00:10:47.160 --> 00:10:51.240
he knows he’ll be facing consequences long
after his release

00:10:51.240 --> 00:10:55.029
and continues to seek a court appeal overturning
his sentence

00:10:55.029 --> 00:11:01.460
or a full pardon and expungement for operating
a business within the bounds of California law.

00:11:01.460 --> 00:11:04.090
What are you planning on doing once you are
out?

00:11:04.090 --> 00:11:07.680
Manly it's filing bankruptcy, I don't know
after that,

00:11:07.680 --> 00:11:10.840
so many things are up in the air, I have five
years of probation,

00:11:10.840 --> 00:11:13.840
I don't know what I'm gonna be allowed to
do.

